{
  "$schema": "https://aka.ms/codetour-schema",
  "title": "How nvim-code-tour works",
  "steps": [
    {
      "description": "### What this is\n\nA minimal player for CodeTour `.tour` files: open the step's file, dim everything except its line or selection, show the description, `]n` and `[n` to move. One Lua module, one command file, no dependencies. This tour walks the pipeline from the command to the screen."
    },
    {
      "file": "plugin/codetour.lua",
      "line": 6,
      "description": "### Entry point\n\n`:CodeTour` with a path starts that tour; without one it lists `.tours/**/*.tour` under the cwd and uses `vim.ui.select`, so whatever picker you already have handles the choice. The command file is deliberately a thin shim: all logic lives in the module so it can be driven from Lua and tests."
    },
    {
      "file": "lua/codetour/init.lua",
      "selection": { "start": { "line": 235, "character": 1 }, "end": { "line": 260, "character": 4 } },
      "description": "### One state table\n\n`start` parses the JSON, builds a single `state` table (tour, root, index, touched buffers) and maps the keys. Everything else reads or mutates that table, and `stop` sets it to nil. The `BufLeave` autocmd closes the float when you wander off, since a float positioned relative to one window makes no sense over another."
    },
    {
      "file": "lua/codetour/init.lua",
      "selection": { "start": { "line": 96, "character": 1 }, "end": { "line": 101, "character": 4 } },
      "description": "### The spotlight\n\nThree extmarks in one namespace: dim from the top of the buffer to the range, highlight the range, dim from the range to the end. Priority 200 on each so the dim wins over treesitter's 100 and `hl_eol` so blank lines dim too. `strict = false` lets an end column past a short line clamp instead of erroring."
    },
    {
      "file": "lua/codetour/init.lua",
      "selection": { "start": { "line": 208, "character": 1 }, "end": { "line": 215, "character": 6 } },
      "description": "### Never edit inside a sidebar\n\nThe first real bug. Running `:edit` while neo-tree is the current window lets neo-tree reopen the file in the main window as a fresh buffer, taking the extmarks and float with the old one. So if the current buffer has a `buftype`, jump to the first normal window in the tab. From the dashboard there is none, and editing in place works there."
    },
    {
      "file": "lua/codetour/init.lua",
      "selection": { "start": { "line": 137, "character": 1 }, "end": { "line": 148, "character": 22 } },
      "description": "### Placing the float\n\nThe float opens at height 1, then asks Neovim for its real rendered height so `linebreak` and `conceal` are accounted for (the earlier estimate left a `@@@` overflow marker). The window is scrolled so the range is centered in the rows the float leaves free, and the float's top border lands on the row after the last highlighted line, aligned with the text column. A range taller than the free rows clamps the float to the bottom."
    },
    {
      "file": "lua/codetour/init.lua",
      "line": 174,
      "description": "### The other mode\n\n`mode = \"virtual\"` renders the same description as `virt_lines` above the anchor instead of a float. The text is word-wrapped by hand because virtual lines do not wrap. Both modes share the spotlight and the scroll; only this call and `show_float` differ."
    },
    {
      "file": "lua/codetour/init.lua",
      "selection": { "start": { "line": 266, "character": 1 }, "end": { "line": 269, "character": 6 } },
      "description": "### Reaching the end\n\n`]n` on the last step ends the tour rather than sticking there. That also unmaps the keys, so a `[n` typed right after falls through to whatever else owns it. Kept on purpose: the tour is a one-shot read, and `<leader>tq` exists for stopping early."
    },
    {
      "description": "### Left out on purpose\n\nNo `pattern` steps, no `directory` reveal, no gutter markers, no statusline, no resume. Unknown fields are ignored so tours written for VS Code still play. If a step's file is missing you get a warning and the description alone. The `skills/` directory holds the agent-facing authoring guide that produced this tour."
    }
  ]
}
